    "payload": {
      "wordMax": 200,
      "wordMin": 180,
      "caseNotes": "\nAnimal:\n'Hugo', Springer Spaniel, male entire, 6 years, 19.4 kg.\nPresented 31 August 2026. This referral written the same day.\nBrought in by his owner, Mr Tom Raines, at the start of morning surgery.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nSudden lethargy, one episode of collapse, pale gums and reluctance to exercise over 48 hours.\nThe pale gums were first noticed by the owner's daughter.\nNormally very active; he would not finish his usual walk two days ago.\nAppetite reduced over the same period.\n\nHistory:\nNo toxin access reported; no zinc, no rodenticide.\nThe garden is fenced and no rodent bait is used at home or on the shoot where he works.\nNo recent travel outside the country.\nHas never travelled abroad and has no pet passport.\nVaccinations current, last given four months ago.\nAnnual booster given without any reaction.\nNo known onion or garlic ingestion.\nNo recent medication of any kind.\nFed the same commercial complete dry diet for several years.\nWorks as a gundog during the shooting season; no outings since February.\nNot used for breeding.\nInsured since he was a puppy.\nA grass seed was removed from between the toes of his left forefoot last summer.\nDental check at his vaccination visit was normal.\n\nExamination:\nQuiet but responsive and able to stand unaided.\nBody condition score 5 out of 9.\nPale to icteric mucous membranes.\nSclera mildly yellow on close inspection.\nTachycardia at 170 beats per minute.\nPulses bounding initially and now weak.\nSplenomegaly on palpation.\nNo pain on abdominal palpation.\nGrade II out of VI flow murmur, consistent with the anaemia.\nRespiratory rate 44 with mild effort.\nLungs clear on auscultation.\nTemperature 39.4 degrees Celsius.\nNo petechiae or ecchymoses seen on the gums or skin.\nNo peripheral lymph node enlargement.\n\nDiagnostics:\nPacked cell volume 14 per cent, with a regenerative response and marked reticulocytosis.\nSpherocytosis on blood smear.\nPositive in-saline agglutination test, persisting after washing.\nAgglutination was also visible to the naked eye on the slide.\nMild hyperbilirubinaemia.\nPlasma visibly yellow-tinged in the haematocrit tube.\nNo haemoparasites seen on smear.\nSNAP tests for vector-borne disease negative.\nThoracic and abdominal imaging showed no mass and no evidence of neoplasia; splenomegaly only.\nPlatelet count normal, so no concurrent thrombocytopenia. With no infectious, drug, toxin or neoplastic trigger found, this appears to be primary (non-associative) IMHA.\n\nCurrent treatment:\nPrednisolone 2 mg/kg per day, started this morning.\nGastroprotectant.\nIntravenous crystalloids.\nHe was quiet but settled during fluid therapy and accepted gentle handling.\nIntravenous catheter in the right cephalic vein, secured and bandaged for travel.\nClopidogrel for thromboprophylaxis — thromboembolism remains the leading cause of death in this disease and prophylaxis has been started early.\n\nPlan:\nRefer urgently for blood typing and packed red cell transfusion; the packed cell volume is falling and he is symptomatic at rest.\nConsideration of second-line immunosuppression will be needed if there is no response within the first few days.\nThe owner is aware of the guarded prognosis and of the cost of transfusion and prolonged hospitalisation, and wishes to proceed.\nHe is travelling with the dog now and has our full records.\nThe records include the full blood results, the smear report and the imaging report.\n\nOwner and household:\nLives with Mr Raines, his wife and their two teenage children; no other pets.\nMr Raines asked whether the vaccination could have caused this and was told it is unlikely given the four-month gap.\nHe asked whether the children should be kept away and was reassured that the condition is not contagious.\nHe understands that blood typing is needed before transfusion and that more than one transfusion may be required.\nHe would like a daily telephone update, ideally in the early evening.\nThe family had another Springer Spaniel before Hugo, who lived to fourteen.\nHis working season usually starts in October; Mr Raines accepts that he will not be working this year.\nThe shoot captain has been told that Hugo will not be available for now.\nHe has asked his wife to collect the children from school while he travels.\nMr Raines has taken the rest of the week off work so that he can be available.\nHe asked whether Hugo could keep his favourite toy with him in the kennel.\nHe asked whether he could leave his car in the hospital car park overnight if needed.\nHe can be reached on the mobile number on the referral form at any time.\n",
      "recipient": "Dr Nathan Brooks, Veterinary Internal Medicine Specialist, Hartwell Referral Hospital",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Refer this dog for transfusion support and immunosuppressive management of suspected immune-mediated haemolytic anaemia."
    },
    "guidanceNote": "Include the agglutination and spherocyte findings that support the immune-mediated diagnosis, plus the current PCV, so the team can prioritise cross-matching and transfusion."
